# Ground truth for shiftleft-go-demo (formerly GoVWA), a deliberately
# vulnerable Go web application.
#
# The expectations come from the application's own README, which enumerates its
# exploits, checked against the code so each one names the source and sink it
# actually has.
#
# Not every declared exploit is a taint flow, and the ones that are not are
# listed here rather than silently dropped:
#
#   IDOR 1 and IDOR 2 (/idor1, /idor2) are authorization flaws. The SQL they
#   reach is a compile-time constant with ? placeholders, executed through
#   stmt.Exec(name, city, phoneNumber, uid) — properly parameterized. The bug
#   is that the handler never checks the requester owns the profile. No taint
#   analysis should report these, and one that did would be wrong.
#
#   CSA (/csa) is a client-side authorization bypass: the OTP is compared in
#   the browser. There is no server-side flow from an untrusted source to a
#   dangerous sink.
#
#   XXE (vulnerability/xxe) is a stub — the package contains one function that
#   prints "tes". There is nothing to find.
#
# That leaves five real flows. Each is pinned to the handler it belongs to,
# because sqli1/sqli2 and xss1/xss2 reach identical sinks and differ only in
# where the input came from.

# SQLi 1: the Uid cookie is read without sanitisation, formatted into a SELECT
# with fmt.Sprintf in UnsafeQueryGetData, and executed with DB.Query.
want flow source=http-input sink=data sourcefn=~sqli1Handler sinkfn=~Query

# SQLi 2: the same sink, reached from the uid URL parameter instead.
want flow source=http-input sink=data sourcefn=~sqli2Handler sinkfn=~Query

# XSS 1: the search term is wrapped in template.HTML, which marks a string as
# already-safe HTML and so suppresses the escaping that would neutralise it.
# known-fail=legacy:34: template.HTML is a named string type, so this is a
# conversion and not a call. Legacy models it as a function sink, which can
# never match; SEAM matches the conversion (models entries with
# match="conversion").
want flow source=http-input sink=template-injection sourcefn=~xss1Handler known-fail=legacy:34

# XSS 2: the uid URL parameter is interpolated into an inline <script> body and
# marked safe the same way.
want flow source=http-input sink=template-injection sourcefn=~xss2Handler known-fail=legacy:34

# Zip slip: the request body is opened as an archive and extracted to disk with
# a path taken from the archive's own entries. The sink is Extract on a
# third-party archive library (github.com/gen2brain/go-unarr). The tainted
# value is the archive itself, so the model marks the receiver relevant rather
# than any argument, and the category is zip-slip.
#
# SEAM finds this flow. It did not until the SSA builder stopped skipping
# packages that inherit IllTyped from a dependency: go-unarr's `import "C"`
# cannot be resolved by the loader, packages.IllTyped is transitive, and
# ssautil.AllPackages therefore built no bodies for path-traversal even though
# its own type-checking succeeded with no errors. See buildAllPackages in
# internal/analyzer/ssa_context.go.
#
# known-fail=legacy:33: legacy reports no slice anywhere in this repository that
# mentions unarr, so it drops the flow somewhere between the r.Body field read
# and the sink. It is not the sink shape — legacy does honour ReceiverRelevant
# (dataflow.go:1476) and does propagate through *ssa.Extract (dataflow.go:1666).
# The hop that loses it has not been isolated. SEAM's depCrossing count on this
# repository is 54 against legacy's 0, so the likeliest cause is the general
# weakness this marker shares with the other legacy xfails: taint that has to
# cross into a dependency's modelled surface and come back out.
want flow source=http-input sink=zip-slip sinkfn=~Extract known-fail=legacy:33
